course: chinese-street
lesson: 17
title: Hungry, tired, shy, excited
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: You walk into Lín's kitchen after school and say "wǒ è le" — "waw UH luh", I'm hungry — and his mum is delighted: feeding a hungry child is her favourite job.
explain: The little word "le" at the end means "it has happened now": wǒ è le, wǒ kě le, wǒ lèi le, wǒ kùn le — hungry, thirsty, worn out, sleepy.
explain: Tones trick the ear here: è (hungry) falls like a short "uh!", but é rising is "goose" — so a wobbly voice can announce "I'm a goose" instead.
explain: Mandarin splits "tired" in two: lèi is body-tired after football, kùn is eyes-closing sleepy; and "zháojí" mixes worried with in-a-hurry, so a parent who is zháojí is anxious, not angry.
explain: People say "wǒ méi shì" — I'm fine — even when they are not; if your friend's face says otherwise, stay with him.
fact: "wǒ è le" (waw UH luh) means "I'm hungry"; è falls, while é rising means goose, and the friendly answer is "chī diǎnr dōngxi ba", have something to eat.
fact: "wǒ kě le" means "I'm thirsty", and the answer is "hē shuǐ ba", have some water; kě also turns up in kěyǐ (may, can) and kě'ài (cute).
fact: "wǒ lèi le" means "I'm tired, worn out" in the body, after sport or a long walk; the answer is "xiūxi yíxià", have a rest.
fact: "wǒ kùn le" means "I'm sleepy" and the answer is "shuìjiào ba", go to sleep; you can be lèi without being kùn.
fact: "wǒ bù shūfu" means "I don't feel well"; shūfu means comfortable, so "hǎo shūfu" on a soft sofa is "so comfy", and an adult will ask "nǎr bù shūfu?".
fact: "jǐnzhāng" means nervous or tense, before a test or a race; a close match can also be jǐnzhāng, and friends say "bié jǐnzhāng, jiāyóu!".
fact: "hàixiū" means shy; grown-ups say "tā hàixiū" about a quiet child, and a quick "āyí hǎo" to the friend's mum stops them calling you that.
fact: "xīngfèn" means excited, the bubbly can't-sit-still feeling before a birthday, a trip or a festival.
fact: "zháojí" (both syllables rising) means worried and in a hurry at once; "bié zháojí" means "don't worry, take your time".
fact: "wǒ xiǎng nǐ" means "I miss you", said to a friend who moved away or to grandparents on a video call; the answer is "wǒ yě xiǎng nǐ".
fact: xiǎng also means "think" and "want": "wǒ xiǎng chī" means "I want to eat".
fact: "xiǎng jiā" means homesick, on a sleepover or a school trip; grandparents who moved from China may feel xiǎng jiā too.
fact: "wǒ méi shì" means "I'm OK, I'm fine", but people say it even when they are not fine.
exercise: After school at Lín's house you say "wǒ è le". What did you tell his mum? | I'm hungry | I'm thirsty | I'm sleepy | I'm bored
exercise: Lín's mum hears "wǒ è le". What is she likely to say? | chī diǎnr dōngxi ba | shuìjiào ba | bié pà | xiūxi yíxià
exercise: After a race in the park Lín pants "wǒ kě le". What does he want? | A drink | A nap | A snack | A plaster
exercise: Lín's little sister yawns at ten at night: "wǒ kùn le". What does she mean? | I'm sleepy | I'm hungry | I'm worn out from sport | I'm homesick
exercise: After a long football match, which do you say? | wǒ lèi le | wǒ kùn le | wǒ è le | wǒ xiǎng nǐ
exercise: Lín's mum answers "xiūxi yíxià". What is she telling you? | Have a rest | Have a drink | Go to bed now | Wash your hands
exercise: You feel sick at Lín's house. What do you tell his mum? | wǒ bù shūfu | wǒ hǎo shūfu | wǒ xīngfèn | wǒ hàixiū
exercise: Lín flops on a big soft sofa: "hǎo shūfu!". What does he mean? | So comfy! | I feel sick! | So boring! | So scary!
exercise: Before the spelling test Lín's hands shake. Which word fits? | jǐnzhāng | xīngfèn | hàixiū | wúliáo
exercise: You don't greet Lín's mum and hide behind him. She says "tā hàixiū". What does she think? | That you're shy | That you're rude | That you're hungry | That you're ill
exercise: It's the night before Lín's birthday trip and he can't sit still. Which word? | xīngfèn | jǐnzhāng | kùn | nánguò
exercise: Lín's dad is late for work, searching for his keys, and Lín says "tā hěn zháojí". What is Dad like? | Anxious and in a hurry | Very angry with Lín for hiding them | Very sleepy | Very happy to stay at home
exercise: Your friend is fumbling with his shoelaces at the door. What do you say kindly? | bié zháojí | bié shēngqì | wǒ kùn le | wǒ kě le
exercise: On a video call Lín's granny says "wǒ xiǎng nǐ". What does Lín answer? | wǒ yě xiǎng nǐ | wǒ yě è le | wǒ méi shì | wǒ bù shūfu
exercise: On the school trip Lín's quiet and says "xiǎng jiā". What's up? | He's homesick | He wants a snack | He wants to play | He's angry with you
exercise: Lín says "wǒ xiǎng chī bāozi". What does xiǎng mean here? | Want | Miss | Hate | Cook
exercise: You fell over and Lín asks if you're OK. How do you say "I'm fine"? | wǒ méi shì | wǒ bù shūfu | wǒ hǎo pà | wǒ kùn le
test: my mate said "wǒ é le" with his voice going up and Lín cracked up. why? | é rising is "goose", so he said "I'm a goose" | é rising is a rude word you never say at home | é rising means "I'm thirsty" | Lín was just being mean
test: Lín's cousin says lèi and kùn are exactly the same word. Right? | No: lèi is worn out, kùn is sleepy | Yes, both just mean tired, any time | No: lèi is hungry, kùn is thirsty | Yes, but kùn is ruder
test: Lín has just woken up after ten hours' sleep and then runs a cross-country race. Which does he say at the finish? | wǒ lèi le | wǒ kùn le | wǒ xiǎng jiā | wǒ hàixiū
test: Lín's dad keeps checking the clock at the hospital while Granny is seen. Lín whispers "bàba hěn zháojí". Is Dad cross with Lín? | No, he's anxious, not angry | Yes, zháojí means furious | Yes, he's cross with the doctor | No, he's bored
test: Lín says "wǒ méi shì" but his eyes are red and he won't look at you. What should you do? | Stay with him and ask gently | Believe him and go off to play | Laugh, he's being a baby | Tell everyone he's crying
test: why does Lín's mum keep calling me hàixiū, i'm not even shy lol | You don't greet her; "āyí hǎo" fixes it | Because hàixiū means "very polite boy" | Because hàixiū means "hungry child" | Because she thinks you're ill and pale
test: The football final is 2–2 in the last minute. Lín's dad says "hǎo jǐnzhāng!". What does he mean? | It's so tense and exciting! | It's so boring! | I'm so hungry! | It's so comfy!
test: In a cartoon a panda says "wǒ kě le" and is given a sandwich. What's wrong? | kě is thirsty, he needed a drink | Nothing, kě means hungry | kě means sleepy, he needed bed | kě means shy, he needed a hug
test: Which word sits in kě le (thirsty), kěyǐ (may) and kě'ài (cute) — same sound, different jobs? | kě | kùn | è | lèi
test: What would happen if you told Lín's mum "wǒ è le" at teatime? | She'd happily give you something to eat | She'd send you home at once | She'd think you were rude | She'd give you a glass of water only
test: On a sleepover Lín's cousin cries quietly and says "xiǎng jiā". What's the kind thing? | Comfort him; maybe ring his parents | Call him a baby | Tell him to shut up and sleep | Eat his snacks
exam: Identify the utterance whereby a speaker of Mandarin customarily signifies hunger. | wǒ è le | wǒ kě le | wǒ kùn le | wǒ lèi le
exam: It is given that a pupil has slept adequately yet is physically exhausted following athletic exertion. Select the expression most consistent with the established distinction. | wǒ lèi le | wǒ kùn le | wǒ xiǎng jiā | wǒ hàixiū
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the term "zháojí" as applied to a parent? | It denotes anxiety combined with haste, not anger | It denotes ungovernable fury directed at the child | It denotes drowsiness | It denotes shyness in company
exam: Ascertain the meaning of the expression "xiǎng jiā" as employed by a child residing temporarily away from home. | Homesickness, a longing for home | A wish to purchase a house | A desire to eat at once | An intention to telephone a friend
